; @example   run-tests
; @summary   A module of pure functions with an accompanying test file, run by `ainl test`.
; @teaches   test builtin, ainl test, modules, unit tests
; @scope     interpreter-only
; @expect    tested 3
; @run       ainl run examples/corpus/testing/run-tests.ainl
; @test      ainl test examples/corpus/testing/suite
;
; `test` is an ordinary builtin, not a special form and not a separate
; language. `(test name expr expected)` evaluates `expr`, compares the result
; against `expected` **as a string**, and returns `true` — or aborts with a
; message naming the test, the expected value and the actual one.
;
; THE THREE RULES THAT MATTER:
;
;   1. The expectation is a STRING, written in quotes: `"3"`, not `3`. The
;      comparison is textual, which means it works identically on every
;      backend and for every value type — but it also means `"3"` and `3` are
;      the same expectation, and `(test "x" 3.0 "3.0")` is the only way to
;      assert on a float, since floats print with a mandatory `.0`.
;
;   2. A FAILURE IS AN ERROR, not a printed line. That is deliberate. A test
;      that fails and keeps going can leave later assertions asserting on
;      state the failure already invalidated, and it makes "how many passed"
;      a number nobody can trust. The first failure in a file aborts that
;      file; `ainl test` still runs the remaining files and reports the split.
;
;   3. A SUITE IS A DIRECTORY OR A FILE, and the exit code is the contract:
;      0 when every test passed, non-zero otherwise. That is the whole reason
;      the runner exists — a script needs a boolean, not a pretty summary.
;
; WHY THESE LIVE IN A SEPARATE DIRECTORY: the test file imports the module
; under test, so it can exercise pure functions without any of the module's
; I/O. And because `test` is portable, this suite runs on the interpreter,
; the AOT binary and all three transpiler targets.

(import "pure")

; --- the module's own smoke check ------------------------------------------
; A program can assert about itself at run time, not only under `ainl test`.
; That is the difference between the two: `ainl test` is a development tool
; that runs a whole suite and reports a summary; a `(test ...)` at the top
; level is just a builtin call that guards a claim before the program uses it.
(test "square 7" (square 7) "49")
(test "append preserves order" (append (list 1 2) (list 3)) "(1 2 3)")
(test "classify sorts a number" (classify 5) "positive")

; The same module, exercised through the runner.
(print "tested" 3 "assertions inline; the full suite is in suite/")

; Point at the suite so a reader knows exactly what to run:
;   ainl test examples/corpus/testing/suite
(print "run the suite with:" "ainl test examples/corpus/testing/suite")
